<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Invasion of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as September Campaign, Polish Campaign, Polish Defensive War of 1939</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, also known as the September Campaign, was the joint attack on the Republic of Poland by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Slovak Republic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> that marked the beginning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>. German and Slovak forces invaded on 1 September 1939, a week after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> was signed, and on 3 September the United Kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on Germany. After the Polish defeat in the Battle of the Bzura, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded eastern Poland on 17 September under the pact's secret protocol. The campaign ended on 6 October after the Battle of Kock, with Germany and the Soviet Union dividing the whole of Poland, which never formally surrendered.</p></div>
